I wrote the plan for this session and committed it as e32d46bc. Nothing has been implemented yet; that's the next phase.

Task 1 (product, safety): make the hard deny match commands, not substrings. Today the hard-deny list matches with a plain contains, so it fails in both directions:
• **Blocks too much:** rm -rf /tmp/build is refused, and so is any grep or heredoc that only mentions rm -rf /. This assessment was refused twice for exactly that.
• **Misses the real danger:** rm -fr /, rm -r -f / and rm -rf --no-preserve-root / get through.

The plan moves the check into a new src/hard_deny.rs with one function that splits the command into tokens (quoted text counts as an argument, never a command) and looks inside bash -c / sh -c / eval strings so quoting can't be used to sneak past. It covers rules for rm, mkfs and dd. Other entries on the existing list keep their current substring match unless they can be tightened without loosening them. One test table checks both directions: catastrophic spellings must be refused, ordinary commands that look similar must pass. A deliberate break-then-restore run confirms the tests catch it. The parent bash and the sub-agent bash already share this check, so one fix covers both.

Task 2 (product, #982 part 2): `yoyo diff`, `yoyo commit` and `yoyo blame` exit 1 outside a git repo. Each handler gets a version that returns a status code and feeds the existing single exit path. The interactive (REPL) callers stay unchanged. The plan tells the implementer to look first for tests that run these commands inside the test process, because once they call process::exit they would kill the test binary. lint, health, changelog and the find/grep no-match question are left for part 3.

